<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Carlist War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Carlist War (1833–1840) was a Spanish civil war over the succession to Ferdinand VII. Supporters of his brother Carlos, strongest in the Basque provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Catalonia, fought for him and for traditional rights against the liberal regency of Maria Christina for the young Isabella II. The Convention of Vergara ended the fighting in the north in 1839, and the last Carlist forces were defeated in 1840.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war.jpg" alt="First Carlist War" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lyde Hornbrook · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puerta_de_Behobia_de_Irun,_bajo_el_ataque_de_las_fuerzas_Reales_Irlandesas,_el_17_de_mayo_de_1837.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was in deep social, economic and political crisis. Its population was about 12.3 million, and between 1824 and 1833 the state's average annual income was barely more than half its level before the wars. In 1823 Ferdinand VII repealed the Spanish Constitution of 1812, which had abolished the fueros, and in the Ominous Decade that followed he suppressed his enemies and the press with the help of a militia, the Voluntarios Realistas, which reached 284,000 men in 1832.</p><p>The Basque provinces had kept a relative autonomy since supporting Philip V in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. The western Basque provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> sided with Carlos because he was willing to uphold their institutions and laws, though historians disagree on whether the cause there was essentially one for the fueros. The Liberals held the institutions, almost the whole army and the cities, and had the support of the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Portugal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Tomás de Zumalacárregui, the Carlist commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, swore an oath to uphold the fueros and made his base in the Amescoas, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra">Estella</a>, where 3,000 volunteers joined him. In April 1834 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal signed the treaty of the Quadruple Alliance. Both sides shot prisoners; the most notorious case was at Heredia, where 118 Liberal prisoners were executed on Zumalacárregui's order. After his death in 1835 the Liberals slowly regained the initiative. In 1837 the Royal Expedition reached the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> but retreated after the Battle of Aranzueque, while in the east Ramón Cabrera held the initiative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war was the deadliest civil war of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, responsible for the deaths of 5% of the 1833 population, with military casualties about half that number. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> fell from 15,000 to 10,234 inhabitants. In October 1839 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> kept a reduced form of home rule, covering taxation and the military draft, in exchange for their incorporation into a centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1840 General Baldomero Espartero became premier and regent with the support of the Progressives. The war is often compared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> a century later; Mark Lawrence writes that it offered a comparable struggle between legitimist Carlists and modernising Liberals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Carlist War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spanish Navarre kept its separate status for more than three centuries, but after the <b>First Carlist War</b>, in which many Navarrese fought for the Carlist cause, the law of 16 August 1841, known as the Ley Paccionada, turned the kingdom into a province with a special fiscal regime.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
